# 15. CRITICAL FINANCIAL SYSTEM

This is one of the most important parts of the entire platform.

Treat the financial subsystem as a controlled accounting and payment system.

Do not implement it as a collection of simple balance fields.

## 15.1 Accounting architecture

Implement double-entry bookkeeping.

Required entities include:

- Financial accounts.
- Account categories.
- Journal entries.
- Journal lines.
- Financial periods.
- Financial policies.
- Picker earning liabilities.
- Wallet liabilities.
- Buyer receivables.
- Buyer receipts.
- Picker payouts.
- Healthcare liabilities.
- Restricted fund accounts.
- Operating expenses.
- Inventory and cost of goods sold.
- Financial distributions.
- Reconciliations.
- Approval records.
- Audit records.

Every posted journal entry must balance:

Total debits = Total credits.

Enforce this through transactional application logic and suitable database-level safeguards.

Use reversal entries to correct posted accounting entries. Never delete posted financial history.

Use immutable financial event references.

## 15.2 Money and precision

Use:
- UGX as the initial base currency.
- PostgreSQL NUMERIC.
- Prisma Decimal.
- Explicit rounding rules.
- No JavaScript floating-point calculations for money.
- Immutable transaction references.
- Idempotency keys.

Store currency on financial records so that future currency expansion is possible.

Never store money in binary floating-point fields.

## 15.3 Guaranteed picker earnings

This is a core WasteHeals commitment.

A picker receives guaranteed earnings for every accepted kilogram according to the applicable approved material and grade rate.

At acceptance:
- Load the approved purchase rate.
- Calculate earnings using Decimal.
- Save the rate snapshot.
- Create the picker earning entitlement.
- Create the corresponding accounting liability.
- Update inventory atomically.
- Prevent duplicate processing.

The picker must not lose an accepted earning simply because a buyer pays late or a later commercial sale is delayed, provided the picker complied with acceptance conditions.

## 15.4 Picker payment preference

Each picker chooses one of two settlement methods:

A. Direct mobile-money payment.

B. Credit to their WasteHeals wallet, subject to applicable legal and regulatory approval.

The choice must be recorded and applied to each eligible earning.

Do not credit the wallet and initiate a mobile-money payout for the same earning.

Support:
- Payment preference.
- Verified payment destination.
- Payment initiation.
- Pending status.
- Successful status.
- Failed status.
- Unknown status.
- Reconciliation.
- Payout receipts.
- Payment history.

A preference change must not silently alter payments already being processed.

## 15.5 Mobile-money payment architecture

Build a dedicated payment orchestration subsystem.

Support integration adapters for:
- MTN Mobile Money.
- Airtel Money.

The application must be able to add other providers without rewriting financial business logic.

Each payment must have:
- Internal payment ID.
- Unique idempotency key.
- Provider reference.
- Picker reference.
- Amount.
- Currency.
- Destination reference.
- Initiating user or system process.
- Payment purpose.
- Creation timestamp.
- Provider status.
- Final settlement status.
- Failure code and description.
- Callback history.
- Reconciliation status.

Required payment lifecycle:

CREATED → VALIDATING → SUBMITTED → PENDING → SUCCESS / FAILED / UNKNOWN.

The system must handle:
- Duplicate payment requests.
- Duplicate callbacks.
- Delayed callbacks.
- Out-of-order callbacks.
- Network timeouts.
- Provider downtime.
- Insufficient provider balance.
- Incorrect phone numbers.
- Invalid account destinations.
- Rejected transactions.
- Reversed transactions.
- Ambiguous provider responses.

CRITICAL RULE:

A timeout is not proof of failure.

If a provider request times out after submission, mark it UNKNOWN/PENDING RECONCILIATION. Query the provider using the original reference where supported. Never blindly create another payout.

Verify provider callbacks using the provider's documented authentication and signature mechanisms.

Validate:
- Callback authenticity.
- Transaction reference.
- Expected amount.
- Currency.
- Destination.
- Transaction state.
- Duplicate event status.

Never trust a callback simply because it reached an endpoint.

Never expose API secrets in frontend code, application logs or source control.

Never store mobile-money PINs or OTPs.

Use secure secret management and encrypted sensitive destinations.

Implement provider-specific adapters with documented sandbox and production configurations.

A provider payment is not financially successful until its authoritative final result is verified and the corresponding accounting transaction is posted.

## 15.6 Reconciliation

Build a dedicated payment reconciliation engine.

Support:
- Internal payment records.
- Provider transaction records.
- Provider statement imports where available.
- Automatic matching.
- Manual matching.
- Unmatched transactions.
- Duplicate detection.
- Missing callbacks.
- Unexpected amounts.
- Reversals.
- Reconciliation reports.
- Finance approval.

Every payment must eventually be classified as settled, failed, reversed or unresolved.

Unresolved transactions must remain visible to authorised finance staff.

Do not silently clear unresolved payments.

## 15.7 Wallet system

If approved for operation, implement a proper double-entry wallet ledger.

Wallet features:
- Wallet account.
- Wallet balance derived from posted transactions.
- Credits.
- Withdrawals.
- Transfers where legally permitted.
- Transaction history.
- Reversals.
- Limits.
- Suspicious activity monitoring.
- Account suspension.
- Reconciliation.

Wallet balance must never be the sole source of truth.

The wallet ledger is the source of the displayed balance.

Implement withdrawal controls, limits and approval workflows.

Before enabling real stored-value wallet functionality, obtain legal advice on Uganda's applicable payment-services and electronic-money requirements. If authorisation or a licensed partner is required, integrate through that approved structure instead of operating an unlicensed wallet.

## 15.8 Healthcare contribution policy

The approved initial policy is:

UGX 30 per kilogram of waste confirmed as sold.

The contribution is calculated on confirmed sold kilograms, not simply collected kilograms.

For example:

1,000 confirmed sold kilograms × UGX 30 = UGX 30,000 healthcare contribution.

Record:
- Sold quantity.
- Contribution rate.
- Calculated contribution.
- Buyer sale reference.
- Funding status.
- Actual cash funding.
- Fund account.
- Allocation history.

Recognise the obligation when the sale is confirmed under the applicable accounting policy.

Do not report an unfunded contribution as cash available for healthcare.

When buyer cash is received, allocate the appropriate amount to the restricted healthcare fund in accordance with the approved policy.

## 15.9 Performance rewards

30% of positive distributable surplus is allocated to the picker performance reward pool.

The reward pool must be zero if distributable surplus is zero or negative.

Do not calculate picker rewards as 30% of gross sales.

Suggested distribution score:

- Verified accepted kilograms: 50%.
- Quality and sorting accuracy: 25%.
- Consistency: 15%.
- Safety and training compliance: 10%.

Normalise scores and allocate the pool proportionally.

For the initial pilot, support a simpler verified-volume and quality model until sufficient reliable data exists.

Publish reward rules to pickers.

Provide a reward calculation explanation and dispute process.

Do not incentivise unsafe collection practices or penalise legitimate absence arbitrarily.

## 15.10 Surplus allocation

Calculate distributable surplus only after considering the applicable business costs, including:

- Cost of sold inventory.
- Picker procurement costs.
- Transport.
- Sorting and handling.
- Healthcare contributions.
- Operating expenses.
- Rejections and expected losses.
- Applicable taxes and other obligations.
- Required reserves and adjustments.

Do not allocate a surplus if the result is zero or negative.

Initial proposed allocation policy, configurable and subject to approval:

- 30% picker performance rewards.
- 35% operations.
- 20% contingency.
- 15% expansion.

These are configurable policy percentages, not hardcoded constants.

Guaranteed picker earnings are separate from the performance reward pool.

## 15.11 Liquidity protection

Accounting profit does not automatically mean cash is available.

Before any surplus distribution, calculate available distributable cash after accounting for:

- Unsettled picker earnings.
- Due healthcare obligations.
- Due operating obligations.
- Taxes and other liabilities.
- Minimum working capital.
- Required contingency liquidity.

Block or reduce distributions if the liquidity test fails.

Provide an explanation of the failed condition.

## 15.12 Accounting reports

Implement:
- Profit and loss.
- Balance sheet.
- Cash flow.
- Trial balance.
- General ledger.
- Accounts receivable.
- Accounts payable.
- Picker liability report.
- Payout report.
- Wallet liability report.
- Healthcare fund report.
- Revenue by material.
- Cost by material.
- Contribution margin per kilogram.
- Inventory valuation.
- Buyer ageing.
- Financial period closing.
- Financial reconciliation.
- Surplus calculation.

Distinguish accounting profitability from available cash.

